10 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049984 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "23eb9850-2a23-5b4a-945c-ef50f44e8b95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050715 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186171947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050715 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186171948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050715 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186171949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050715 #edb/ref 1031 "Proving Surjectivity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050715 #edb/ref 1033 3422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050715 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "60bbba35-9aeb-5f53-99d7-85c7b3592873" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050753 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050753 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050753 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050753 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050753 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050753 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050753 #edb/ref 1031 "Graphing the Square Root Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050753 #edb/ref 1033 2038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050753 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "63beb30b-812d-5842-a68b-ba473953a810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050829 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050829 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050829 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050829 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050829 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050829 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050829 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050829 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050829 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050829 #edb/ref 1031 "Properties of Transformed Exponential Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050829 #edb/ref 1033 1609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050829 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6a056b25-47a5-57f1-ad81-32b91dee4044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051069 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051069 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051069 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051069 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051069 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051069 #edb/ref 1031 "Into Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051069 #edb/ref 1033 3027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051069 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161754 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8021" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161754 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5c/5c5e52c78f925dc4e01b1098b507865407f9848cc1db6e8a946ad81840e8e4ee.png)\n\n  \n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X \\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(2) = 4$\n\n- $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y$\n\n- The function $f:X \\to Y$ is **into**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161754 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ac70ed6-4f03-59ae-9a3b-b8a93386507d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161754 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed,   there is an arrow from $2 \\in X$ to $4\\in Y.$ So,  $f(2)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $1 \\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $1 \\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=1$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x) \\neq y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161755 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8022" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161755 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-1)^2-2?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[-2, \\infty)$\n\n- $\\forall\\, y \\in [-2, \\infty),$ $\\exists\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\n- $f$ maps $\\mathbb{R}$ **into** $\\mathbb{R}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161755 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "346ff48a-b54f-56cc-b5b2-4437c7d3d94d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161755 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/35949b4a4809821871a9edf9466fa00862778094d754674014407525b5fc2fed.png)\n\nRecall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $-2,$ and it occurs at the point $(1,-2).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values higher than $y=-2$, including $y=-2$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $[-2, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $[-2, \\infty)$, then for all $y$ contained within $[-2, \\infty),$ there exists some $x$ contained within $\\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x) = y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=-3 \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ So, $f$ maps $\\mathbb{R}$ *into* $\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II,  and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161756 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161756 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\n- $f: [-2, \\infty) \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(x) =\\sqrt{ x+2}$\n\n- $g: \\mathbb{R} \\to [0, \\infty)$ defined by $g(x) = |x-2|+3$\n\n- $h:(0,\\infty) \\to\\Bbb R$ defined by $h(x) = \\ln(x)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161756 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d3e8ba8d-5a83-5683-9e19-33f021459ceb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161756 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is an into function. Let's take $-1 \\in\\Bbb R.$ There is no number $x \\in[-2, \\infty)$ such that $f(x) =\\sqrt{ x+2}=-1.$ In fact, $f(x) =\\sqrt{ x+2}  \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in[-2, \\infty) .$\n\n- II is an into function. Let's take $1 \\in [0, \\infty).$ There is no number $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$  such that $g(x)=|x-2|+3=1.$ In fact, $|x-2|+3 \\geq 3$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- III is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y \\in \\Bbb R,$ we can choose $x =e^{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Then $h(x) = h \\left( e^{y}\\right) =\\ln(e^{y}) = y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161920 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161920 #edb/ref 1039 "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161920 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161920 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161921 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161921 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161921 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161921 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161921 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161921 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161921 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161921 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161921 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161921 #edb/ref 1039 "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161921 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161921 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161922 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161922 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161922 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161922 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161922 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161922 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161922 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161922 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161922 #edb/ref 1039 "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161922 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161922 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162180 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162180 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162180 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162180 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162180 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162180 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162180 #edb/ref 1045 "Into Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162180 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162180 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162180 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162180 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "dc843eb0-59e2-5d15-bd81-c09a747c8de0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162235 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162235 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162235 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162235 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b3/b3c5df109b00450c20acdbc07378bfe309cf808c1244bedd6ff4fa3a850ccd13.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(10)=25$\n- $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n- The function $f:X\\to Y$ is not **into**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162235 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10b365a7-4952-5fcd-929e-00199380cb55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162235 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed,   there is an arrow from $10\\in X$ to $25\\in Y.$ So,  $f(10)=25.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, $f$ is *not* an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162567 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162567 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90713" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162567 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162567 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\n- $f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$\n- $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{4}$\n- $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={5}^{x}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162567 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93bb691b-3476-50ac-a75b-0f3bd8e04d2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162567 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x={log}_{2}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({log}_{2}⁡y)={2}^{{log}_{2}⁡y}=y.$\n\n- II is an into function. Let's take $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only numbers $x$ that satisfy $g(x)={x}^{4}=2$ are $x=\\pm \\sqrt[4]{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={5}^{x}=-1.$ In fact, ${5}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162568 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162568 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90846" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162568 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162568 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)={3}^{x}?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $(0,\\infty )$\n- $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162568 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "615b6ed0-1120-5f2d-a6c0-2c676050f1ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162568 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/a2/a2a6352a60b420f8721d6c7e4553d3157cfb045a39b6a5119c11c5c8189023e2.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the function takes all $y$-values greater than $y=0$, excluding $y=0$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Take, for example, $y=-1.$ Since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ),$ there can' t be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the function is not a surjection.  So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162739 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162739 #edb/ref 1061 181734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162739 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e005dc62-0efa-54ee-906c-34a5e9d6c724" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162739 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162740 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162740 #edb/ref 1061 181735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162740 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b33a7a7-bad5-529c-8e89-89c564737c01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162740 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162741 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162741 #edb/ref 1061 181738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162741 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38c04fef-4547-57e5-ac3a-6aee5d534539" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162741 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162742 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162742 #edb/ref 1061 181741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162742 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c77caab9-b4ab-5f2f-a6d4-b0ba66aacd5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162742 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162743 #edb/ref 1061 181742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162743 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e11e568-ff34-53f6-aaf4-e4c7a0a71c7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162743 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163212 #edb/ref 1064 "A function $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is said to be an  **into** function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write: \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$\n\nThe following diagram shows an example of an into function. \n\n![](images/ff/ff2c2b4099fc1cf30beb2d0f795049e55ad955b06a298d0bd93d8724e22cd0e3.png)\n\n  \n\n According to the diagram,  the element $4 \\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $4.$So, we conclude that there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=4$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x) \\neq y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\nOn the other hand, the following is *not* an into function because every element in $Y$ has at least one element of $X$ that maps to it. \n\n![](images/12/120185557c1f1e2846dece02a4675c2952d1e6c3a01cd66a17458596302266ac.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163212 #edb/ref 1065 4820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163212 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60b69e68-4b54-5b90-ad74-cf6aa0dadc52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163212 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163213 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$\n\nConsider the function $f: \\Bbb R \\rightarrow  [0,\\infty)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=|x-1|+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the codomain is not $\\mathbb R$ but $[0,\\infty) \\subseteq \\mathbb R.$ Let's show that $f$ is an into function.  \n\nTo show that $f$ is into $[0,\\infty),$ let's take ${\\color{blue}0} \\in [0, \\infty).$ Notice that there is no number $x$ in the domain $\\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n$$\ng(x)=|x-1|+1={\\color{blue}0}.\n$$\n\n \nIn fact, $|x-1|+1 \\geq 1$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$      \n\nThus, we have showed that $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ (e.g., $y={\\color{blue}0}$) such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$ Therefore, $f$ is an into function. \n\nThe graph of our function $f$  is shown below.\n\n![](images/11/110203c5a05e9e1b56bbd8483bd78e1be9245a7f1b73738e22ce469ce44f3af1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163213 #edb/ref 1065 4821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163213 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "91165645-9f3f-5d72-a411-e1b6b0b0a3b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163213 #edb/ref 1067 "Cases Where the Codomain Is a Real Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409928 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409928 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409928 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05cadc09-da0d-5ae7-aeaf-a7c3e35f4b2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409929 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409929 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409929 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07dec380-033c-5dbf-a5f5-d2f352afa594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409930 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409930 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409930 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0af2f699-18f8-5488-b57d-5bedd5d91355" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409931 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409931 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409931 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c43997e-0ae2-54ee-8b77-f5d270f7bf43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409932 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409932 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409932 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e10e89a-3526-5277-97f0-adb1bf3a9d69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409933 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409933 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409933 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ee280af-f80a-5d65-a83c-f42a485b1769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409934 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409934 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409934 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14fbca50-5fc0-5de1-bc35-ddb30ff5e841" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409935 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409935 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409935 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "16e59bd1-b50e-5bf9